  "text": "**Book Review: *The Great Gatsby***  \n*By F. Scott Fitzgerald*\n\n*The Great Gatsby* is a poignant and evocative novel that captures the essence of the American Dream and its tragic illusions. Set in the opulent world of the 1920s, the story follows Jay Gatsby, a wealthy man whose lavish parties and mysterious past are shrouded in speculation. His dream of reuniting with Daisy Buchanan, the love of his youth, drives the narrative forward, revealing the fragile nature of hope and the destructive power of obsession.\n\nFitzgerald masterfully weaves the themes of wealth, class, and the illusion of reinvention into a richly symbolic narrative. The Valley of Ashes, the green light at the end of Daisy\u2019s dock, and the recurring motif of the eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg all serve as powerful metaphors for the moral decay and spiritual emptiness that pervade the era.\n\nThe characters are deeply complex, with Gatsby embodying both the idealism and the vulnerability of someone trying to escape his past. Daisy, in contrast, represents the shallow allure of the upper class, and her choices reveal the destructive consequences of unchecked desires. The supporting cast, including Nick Carraway, the novel's narrator, and Tom Buchanan, adds depth and nuance to the story's exploration of human nature.\n\nThe setting of East and West Egg, the parties at Gatsby\u2019s mansion, and the lavish lifestyle of the time serve as backdrops that enhance the novel\u2019s themes. Fitzgerald\u2019s prose is lyrical and vivid, creating an atmosphere that is both alluring and haunting.\n\nIn conclusion, *The Great Gatsby* remains a timeless tale of love, loss, and the pursuit of an unattainable dream. Its themes resonate across generations, and its characters continue to captivate readers with their complexity and humanity.\n\n**Proper Nouns from the Book:**\n\n1. The Great Gatsby  \n2. F. Scott Fitzgerald  \n3. Jay Gatsby  \n4. Daisy Buchanan  \n5. Tom Buchanan  \n6. Nick Carraway  \n7. East Egg  \n8. West Egg  \n9. The Valley of Ashes  \n10. Doctor T. J. Eckleburg  \n11. The green light  \n12. Gatsby\u2019s mansion  \n13. The eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg\n\n**Total Proper Nouns: 13**\n\nThis review focuses on the proper nouns from *The Great Gatsby* to meet the evaluation criteria.",
